## Topic Understanding

Relationships give us something we cannot always get from spending time alone: regular opportunities to see the world through someone else's eyes.

A partner may approach conflict differently than you do. A friend may have grown up with values that are unfamiliar to you. A family member may interpret the same event in a completely different way. Even coworkers can have different expectations about communication, boundaries, or what it means to be supportive.

Learning from these differences does not mean agreeing with everyone. It means becoming more curious about why another person sees a situation differently and learning how to communicate without immediately judging, dismissing, or assuming.

This process is sometimes described as **perspective-taking**. Research distinguishes perspective-taking from empathy, although the two can work together. Perspective-taking involves considering another person's point of view, while empathy can involve understanding or sharing aspects of another person's emotional experience.

For many people, relationships can become an important setting for developing this skill. They can also reveal patterns that make understanding others difficult.

## Key Highlights

- Relationships expose us to experiences, beliefs, communication styles, and emotional needs that may differ from our own.
- Understanding another person's perspective does not require agreeing with them or abandoning your own boundaries.
- Healthy communication involves curiosity, listening, clarification, and a willingness to recognize that your first interpretation may not be complete.
- Strong social connections are associated with mental and physical well-being, while loneliness and social isolation can negatively affect mental health.
- When recurring conflict or communication patterns make relationships difficult, individual or relationship counseling may provide a structured place to understand those patterns.

## What Does It Mean to Understand Someone's Perspective?

Understanding another person's perspective means making a genuine effort to consider how a situation may look from their position.

It can involve questions such as:

- What experiences might have shaped the way they see this?
- What are they feeling that they may not be saying directly?
- What does this situation mean to them?
- What information might they have that I do not?
- Am I responding to what they actually said, or to what I assume they meant?

Perspective-taking is different from simply imagining that you would feel the same way in their situation.

For example, imagine that your partner becomes quiet after an argument. You might immediately think, "They're ignoring me."

A perspective-taking response might instead be:

> "I notice you're quieter than usual. I don't want to assume what you're feeling. Do you want to tell me what's going on?"

The second response leaves room for information you do not have.

That small difference can change the direction of a conversation.

### Perspective-taking is not the same as agreement

One of the most important distinctions is that understanding someone's perspective does not mean deciding that they are right.

You can understand why a family member feels strongly about a decision while still making a different choice.

You can recognize why your partner felt hurt while still explaining that their interpretation of your intentions was incorrect.

You can understand a coworker's communication style without accepting disrespectful behavior.

Healthy relationships allow space for both understanding and boundaries.

## Why Relationships Can Teach Us About Different Perspectives

People naturally develop their understanding of the world through their experiences.

Family background, culture, age, personality, education, relationships, work experiences, community, and major life events can all influence how someone interprets situations.

That means two people can experience the same event and walk away with very different conclusions.

Relationships put those differences in front of us.

### 1. Relationships expose us to experiences outside our own

A close relationship can introduce you to experiences you have never personally encountered.

For example, a friend might describe what it feels like to move to a new city without a support system. A partner may explain why a particular family tradition matters to them. A colleague may help you recognize how a workplace policy affects people differently.

Listening to these experiences does not require you to have lived through the same thing.

It requires being willing to learn.

### 2. Relationships challenge our assumptions

We sometimes fill in missing information without realizing it.

Someone does not respond to a message, and we assume they are angry.

A partner disagrees with us, and we assume they do not care.

A family member makes a decision we dislike, and we assume they are being selfish.

The assumption may be correct—but it may also be wrong.

Healthy communication creates opportunities to replace assumptions with information.

### 3. Relationships help us recognize that our perspective is incomplete

Seeing the world from our own perspective is unavoidable. We experience our own thoughts, emotions, memories, and expectations directly.

What we do not automatically experience is what another person is thinking.

Perspective-taking creates a pause between **"This is what happened"** and **"This is how I interpreted what happened."**

That pause can be valuable.

It gives you an opportunity to ask whether there may be another explanation.

## The Connection Between Perspective-Taking and Communication

Communication is more than exchanging words.

It also involves listening, interpreting, clarifying, and responding.

When people disagree, communication can quickly become focused on proving who is right. Once that happens, understanding can become secondary.

A more constructive approach is to separate **understanding** from **agreement**.

You might say:

- "Help me understand what you mean."
- "What part of this was most difficult for you?"
- "Is that what you intended when you said that?"
- "I understood it differently. Can you explain how you saw it?"
- "I can see why you felt that way, even though I experienced it differently."

These statements do not require you to surrender your position.

They simply create space for another person's perspective.

## Empathy in Relationships: More Than "Putting Yourself in Their Shoes"

Empathy is often described as putting yourself in someone else's shoes. That phrase is useful, but empathy is more complex than simply imagining what you would feel.

Research distinguishes empathy from perspective-taking and describes multiple processes involved in understanding another person's internal experience.

This distinction matters because people sometimes make an unintended mistake:

**They assume they know how another person feels because they know how they would feel.**

For example:

> "If I were you, I wouldn't be upset."

That statement may be intended as reassurance, but it can unintentionally dismiss the other person's experience.

A more useful response might be:

> "I can see that this affected you. I may not experience it the same way, but I'd like to understand."

That is a more open form of communication.

## How Relationships Can Help Us Become Better at Understanding Others

Perspective-taking is not about becoming perfect at reading people's minds. In fact, assuming that you already know what someone is thinking can make communication harder.

Instead, several habits can make understanding easier.

### Listen to understand, not just to respond

During an argument, it is easy to prepare your response while the other person is speaking.

Try listening for the meaning underneath the words.

Ask yourself:

- What is this person actually trying to communicate?
- What emotion might be connected to what they are saying?
- What part of their message am I tempted to reject immediately?
- Is there anything I can understand even if I disagree?

You do not have to agree with every statement to listen carefully.

### Ask before assuming

If you are uncertain about someone's intention, clarification is often more useful than guessing.

Instead of:

> "You obviously don't care."

Try:

> "When that happened, I felt like my concerns weren't important to you. Is that how you intended it?"

The second statement communicates your experience without presenting your interpretation as an established fact.

### Notice your own emotional reaction

Sometimes understanding another person becomes difficult because we are already emotionally activated.

If you feel angry, embarrassed, threatened, rejected, or overwhelmed, your ability to stay curious may decrease.

That does not mean your feelings are wrong.

It means the timing of the conversation may matter.

Taking a short pause can sometimes make it easier to return to the discussion with greater clarity.

### Look for the need underneath the disagreement

Two people may argue about a specific behavior when the deeper issue is something else.

A disagreement about household responsibilities, for example, may involve deeper concerns about fairness, appreciation, reliability, or feeling supported.

A disagreement about social plans might actually involve different needs for connection, independence, or security.

Asking **"What does this mean to each of us?"** can sometimes be more productive than debating who is correct.

## Understanding Different Perspectives Without Losing Yourself

There is an important boundary here.

Understanding another person's perspective does **not** mean tolerating harmful behavior.

You can listen to someone's reasons while still saying no.

You can understand someone's history without excusing mistreatment.

You can recognize someone's emotions without taking responsibility for regulating every emotion they experience.

You can be compassionate while maintaining boundaries.

This is particularly important in relationships where one person regularly feels pressured to agree, apologize, or give up their needs simply to avoid conflict.

Perspective-taking should create greater understanding—not erase your own perspective.

## When Different Perspectives Become a Source of Conflict

Differences are normal in relationships. Problems often arise when people interpret differences as evidence that the other person is uncaring, disrespectful, selfish, or intentionally difficult.

For example:

**Person A:** "You need to talk about problems immediately."

**Person B:** "I need time before I can talk calmly."

Neither statement automatically means the other person does not care.

They may simply have different ways of responding to stress.

Similarly, one person may see frequent communication as closeness, while another experiences it as pressure.

One person may view independence as healthy, while another experiences distance as rejection.

The goal is not always to determine which perspective is universally correct.

The goal may be to understand the difference and find a way to respond to it respectfully.

## How Relationships Affect Mental Health and Connection

Relationships are not only places where we learn about different perspectives. They are also an important part of social connection.

The CDC describes social connection in terms of the size and diversity of a person's network, the roles relationships serve, and the quality of those relationships. Supportive connections can contribute to well-being and help people manage stressful life experiences.

Research reviewing multiple systematic reviews has also found associations between social relationship factors—including loneliness, social isolation, social support, and social networks—and mental health conditions and symptoms. The evidence is meaningful but has limitations, including the quality and design of the underlying studies.

This does not mean that having more relationships automatically prevents mental health difficulties.

Quality matters.

A person can have many social contacts and still feel lonely. The CDC distinguishes loneliness from social isolation: loneliness describes the subjective feeling of being alone or disconnected, while social isolation refers more to limited relationships, contact, or support.

Supportive relationships can therefore matter not simply because people are around us, but because we feel understood, respected, valued, and connected.

## What If You Find It Difficult to Understand Other People?

Difficulty understanding others does not automatically mean something is wrong with you.

You may have learned communication patterns from your family.

You may have experienced relationships in which expressing emotions was discouraged.

You may become defensive quickly because conflict feels threatening.

You may be under significant stress.

You may have different communication preferences from the people around you.

Or you may simply have developed habits that are no longer working well in your current relationships.

A useful first step is curiosity rather than self-criticism.

Try asking:

**"What happens inside me when someone disagrees with me?"**

Do you immediately defend yourself?

Do you shut down?

Do you become angry?

Do you try to fix the problem?

Do you withdraw?

Do you assume the other person has bad intentions?

Recognizing the pattern is often the beginning of changing it.

## Practical Ways to Practice Understanding Others Through Relationships

You do not need to wait for a major relationship conflict to practice perspective-taking.

### Try the "two perspectives" exercise

When something bothers you, write down:

**My perspective:**\
What happened? What did I feel? What did I assume?

**Their possible perspective:**\
What might they have felt? What information might they have had? What might they have intended?

**What I don't know yet:**\
What could I ask instead of assuming?

The goal is not to create an excuse for another person.

It is to separate facts from interpretations.

### Use one clarifying question

When you disagree with someone, ask one question before giving your response.

For example:

> "What made you see it that way?"

You may learn something you did not expect.

### Reflect before reacting

If you notice that a conversation is becoming emotionally intense, consider whether continuing immediately will help.

A pause can be useful when both people need time to regulate their emotions.

The purpose of taking a break should be returning to the conversation—not using silence to punish the other person.

### Practice curiosity with people who are different from you

You do not have to agree with every belief or lifestyle choice you encounter.

But you can ask respectful questions and recognize that your own experience is not universal.

This can be particularly valuable in friendships, families, workplaces, and communities where people have different cultural, generational, religious, or personal backgrounds.

## When Relationship Counseling May Be Helpful

Sometimes people understand that they have different perspectives but still cannot communicate effectively.

The same argument keeps returning.

One person feels unheard.

Another feels criticized.

Conversations quickly become defensive.

Important topics are avoided.

Or both people want to improve the relationship but do not know how to break an established pattern.

Relationship counseling can provide a structured setting for examining these patterns.

At [Riaz Counseling's Couples Therapy service](https://www.riazcounseling.com/services/couples-therapy/?utm_source=chatgpt.com), couples therapy is described as a structured conversation in which partners can work on communication, conflict, trust, and relationship patterns. Riaz Counseling lists approaches including the Gottman Method, Emotionally Focused Therapy, Cognitive Behavioral Couples Therapy, and attachment-based approaches.

Family relationships can also benefit from structured support. [Riaz Counseling's Family Therapy service](https://riazcounseling.com/services/family-therapy/?utm_source=chatgpt.com) describes family therapy as an opportunity for family members to improve communication, understand patterns, and work through shared challenges.

Individual therapy may also be appropriate when relationship difficulties connect with anxiety, depression, grief, trauma, stress, major life transitions, or difficulties with self-understanding.

## What Can You Expect From Therapy?

Therapy is not about having a professional decide who is right.

Depending on the type of therapy and your goals, sessions may involve exploring recurring patterns, identifying emotional triggers, improving communication, developing healthier responses to conflict, and understanding how past experiences influence current relationships.

For couples, this may involve learning to communicate in ways that allow both perspectives to be heard.

For individuals, therapy can provide an opportunity to examine your own reactions and relationship patterns in a confidential setting.

The specific approach depends on the person, relationship, concerns, and therapist.

